 {"id": "PERF-2", "title": "[P3] `lore audit` re-reads and re-parses the ENTIRE validate-history ledger once per registry class (14 full passes): 144 ms at today's 3,161-row ledger, 1.06 s at 10x, 9.56 s at 100x — a single pass is 3.8x (10x: 11.5x) cheaper for the same rows", "status": "complete", "priority": "P3", "created_by": "lore-dogfood-run14", "reasoning": "MEASURED at HEAD a6984b4 on the control box (loadavg 2.9-4.6), the repo's own live ledger as the input: .lore/validate-history.jsonl = 3,161 rows / 878,746 bytes / 18 days / ~176 rows written per day by this product's own `validate --execute` (census: docs/qa/evidence/run14/ledger-census.txt). Rows were resized in scratch dirs (builder /tmp/lore-df14/mkhistory.py) keeping the exact record shape.\n\nCOMMAND (hyperfine --warmup 3 --runs 12, cwd = the dir holding ./.lore/validate-history.jsonl), HEAD build:\n  <repo>/.venv/bin/python -m lore audit\n  ledger rows       audit mean +- sigma     consult (same ledger)\n  0 (no ledger)     48.3 ms +-  2.5 ms      53.9 ms +- 5.5 ms\n  3,161 (today)    144.2 ms +- 15.7 ms      59.4 ms +- 3.1 ms\n  31,610 (10x)       1.056 s +- 0.151 s    116.8 ms +- 4.9 ms\n  316,100 (100x)     9.564 s +- 0.388 s    not measured (--runs 3)\nCOLD (sync + /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches before each run, 3,161-row ledger): audit real 0.23 s / 0.24 s vs warm 0.13 s; consult cold 0.15 s vs warm 0.05 s; control cold `cat` of the ledger 0.03 s. The ledger-dependent part is therefore ~88 ms warm today and ~1.0 s at 10x.\n\nPROFILE (cProfile -s tottime -m lore audit, 3,161-row ledger): __main__.py:560(_audit_rows) 0.200 s cum; validate.py:1194(last_local_validation) called 14 times, 44,254 json.loads, 14 readlines(). At 31,610 rows the same call site reads 1.974 s cum / 442,540 json.loads — i.e. 14 full copies of the file parsed per audit, and the parse work IS the cost.\n\nHOT PATH: lore/__main__.py:604 (`rec = last_local_validation(rb.class_id, path=history_path)` inside the one-row-per-class loop of `_audit_rows`) -> lore/validate.py:1208-1226 (`fh.readlines()` over the WHOLE ledger, then `json.loads` every non-blank line, keeping the last match for that one class_id). `consult`/`compile` read the same file ONCE (lore/__main__.py:75) and only double at 10x (53.9 -> 116.8 ms) — same mechanism, 1x multiplier.\n\nEQUIVALENT WORK, measured (single-pass bench /tmp/lore-df14/passbench.py: read once + parse + keep newest per class, vs the current 14 passes):\n  3,161 rows:   38.0 ms +-  3.5  (1 pass)  vs  145.3 ms +- 28.1  (14 passes)  = 3.8x\n  31,610 rows:  88.2 ms +-  4.7  (1 pass)  vs   1.010 s +- 0.062 (14 passes)  = 11.5x\nA single pass would put audit near floor+one-pass (~86 ms today, ~136 ms at 10x) instead of 144 ms / 1.06 s. This is a projection from the equivalent-work bench, not an applied patch — this lane does not edit code.\n\nWHY A USER FEELS IT: 144 ms today is under the notice threshold; at the file's own measured ~176 rows/day it reaches 31,610 rows in ~5.5 months, where every `lore audit` costs ~1 s (and ~9.5 s at ~4.5 years). Growth is linear and unbounded: no rotation, compaction or cap exists, and no doc mentions the file's growth (docs/INSTALL.md documents only that the file is per-CWD and git-ignored).\n\nFIX DIRECTION: read the ledger ONCE per invocation — build newest-record-per-class_id and pass the mapping into the audit row loop (or memoise last_local_validation per resolved path). Preserve the CURRENT semantics exactly: the newest record wins by FILE ORDER (the scan keeps the last matching line, not max(timestamp)) — probed live, 4 arms, docs/qa/evidence/run14/freshness-arms.txt (clean-then-drifted => stale; drifted-then-clean => proposal). A pruning/rotation knob (or a documented cap) would bound the file itself.\n\nACCEPTANCE: same command, same 3,161-row ledger, warm audit mean <= 100 ms (floor 48 ms + one pass ~38 ms) with `lore audit --format json` unchanged for all 14 rows; the four freshness arms unchanged; a 10x ledger audit <= 250 ms (vs 1.056 s now).\n\nch:trace row=PERF-2 spec=docs/PRD.md#us-3-audit-freshness test=none:no-test-covers-ledger-read-cost evidence=docs/qa/evidence/run14/hyperfine-audit-x10.json witness=dogfood-run14-perf-leg verdict=none:not-judged commit=none:filed-before-fix memory=/dogfood/2026-10-20-lore-run14", "capability_tags": ["perf", "cli", "python"], "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-10-20T08:14:39.551159+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-22T01:57:44.611461-05:00", "detail": {"evidence": [{"kind": "gitreins-verdict", "id": "1e4c0570", "result": "PASS", "perf": "3165-row synthetic ledger: in-process _audit_rows mean=6.07ms (budget ~50ms); CLI python -m lore audit mean=56.6ms (budget <100ms)", "equivalence": "output identical to pre-change multi-pass baseline on a ledger containing torn/blank/non-dict lines; batch==multipass 14/14 classes", "gates": "544 passed; ruff check clean; ruff format 213 files clean; count-guard PASS canonical=544"}, {"kind": "live-measure", "ledger_rows": 4060, "in_process_ms": 7.8, "cli_mean_ms": 60.8, "note": "live ledger grew past the row's 3,161-row input; still 7.8ms in-process"}, {"kind": "hosted-ci", "repo": "get-h3/lore", "head": "df800c6", "conclusion": "success"}]}, "foreman_note": "Filed by the lore-dogfood lane, run 14 (2026-10-20), angle: performance of the audit/consult read path as the local validate ledger grows — the first perf run since PERF-1 (compile_all serialization). No code was changed by this lane. Related pending rows this run re-confirmed: LORE-061 (stale-by-environment rendering) and LORE-065.", "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "df800c6", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "completed_at": "2026-10-22T01:57:44.611461-05:00"}
 {"id": "LORE-066", "title": "[P4] `validate --execute` persists a 0-check 'lint-verified' history record for the unclassified BUCKET on every run: 225 of 3,161 ledger rows (7.1%) carry no evidence yet claim 'commands parse and answer on the live system'", "status": "complete", "priority": "P4", "created_by": "lore-dogfood-run14", "reasoning": "REPRO at HEAD a6984b4, scratch CWD (no repo state touched):\n  mkdir -p /tmp/probe-e && cd /tmp/probe-e\n  <repo>/.venv/bin/python -m lore validate --class unclassified --execute      # rc=0\n  cat .lore/validate-history.jsonl\n  -> {\"class_id\": \"unclassified\", \"drifted_orders\": [], \"executed_count\": 0, \"honesty_label\": \"lint-verified: commands parse and answer on the live system; this does NOT prove recovery succeeds\", \"outcomes\": [], \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-20T08:09:51+00:00\", \"total_checks\": 0}\n\nFINDING: `validate --execute` persists one history record per registry entry, including the `unclassified` BUCKET. The bucket has zero checks by construction (LORE-051: it is explicitly NOT a 14th curated class — audit marks it `\"bucket\": true` in every format), so its record always carries total_checks 0 / executed_count 0 / outcomes [] while its honesty_label still asserts \"commands parse and answer on the live system\". In this repo's own live ledger 225 of 3,161 rows (7.1%) are exactly that record (evidence: docs/qa/evidence/run14/ledger-census.txt, unclassified_rows=225 across 225 batches of 14; batch membership re-verified by grouping timestamps — the 01:48:39 batch carries all 14 ids including unclassified).\n\nIMPACT: (a) 7.1% of every ledger read is weight carrying no evidence, in a file `lore audit` parses once per class (PERF-2, filed the same run); (b) the record claims lint verification for a run in which no command existed — the label-overstatement class the LORE-045/050 honesty work removed elsewhere. No functional harm: audit's freshness path reads that record and finds no drift (drifted_orders [] pairs with zero drift outcomes), so the bucket never flips stale.\n\nFIX DIRECTION (one of): do not persist a history record for an entry with zero checks, or keep the row and make its label derive from total_checks (an honest \"0 checks — bucket, nothing to lint\" instead of the lint-verified claim). Neither may change any curated class's record or the audit row set.\n\nACCEPTANCE: after the fix, `validate --execute` in a scratch CWD either writes 13 records with no `class_id: unclassified` line, or writes the bucket line with a label that does not claim commands answered; `lore audit --format json` still carries the bucket row with `\"bucket\": true` and 13 curated rows byte-identical; a test pins the new shape.\n\nch:trace row=LORE-066 spec=docs/PRD.md#us-3-audit test=none:no-test-covers-bucket-history-row evidence=docs/qa/evidence/run14/bucket-validate-execute-record.jsonl witness=dogfood-run14-local-leg verdict=none:not-judged commit=none:filed-before-fix memory=/dogfood/2026-10-20-lore-run14", "capability_tags": ["python", "verification"], "complexity": 1, "created_at": "2026-10-20T08:14:39.551159+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-22T20:11:56Z", "detail": {"evidence": [{"run_id": ".gitreins/history/2026-10-22/a8860938", "ts": "2026-10-22T20:11:56Z"}]}, "foreman_note": "Filed by the lore-dogfood lane, run 14 (2026-10-20), same tick as PERF-2 — the two rows touch the same file (the bucket row is 7.1% of what PERF-2 measures audit parsing). LORE-051 (bucket distinguished in audit output) is complete and its contract is NOT in question here; this row is scoped to the history-record write path only.\n\nTick lore-foreman-2026-10-22-20-02-54: stale-row recovery, no worker dispatched. Live scratch probe returned rc=0, history lines=0, unclassified=0; audit JSON retained one bucket row plus 13 curated rows. GitReins verdict a8860938 PASS on commit 80c602e (Tier-1 and Tier-2); focused tests 23 passed; count guard PASS canonical=560/live=560; recent CI runs GREEN.", "acceptance_criteria": "ACCEPTANCE (pick branch, state it): in a scratch CWD run lore validate for the unclassified bucket with execution enabled (the selection named in `lore validate --help`), then count the scratch ledger: `grep -c unclassified .lore/validate-history.jsonl` expect (a) 0 new bucket record from that run, or (b) 1 record whose label is the honest zero-checks text with `grep -c 'lint-verified' .lore/validate-history.jsonl` on the bucket line printing 0. Curated classes' records stay byte-identical (`head`-snapshot equality); a test pins the chosen shape; quote the scratch evidence.", "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "80c602e", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "Verification-only close: implementation already landed in 80c602e and is an ancestor of origin/main. persist_validate_history skips zero-check reports, preserving curated records; tests/test_lore066_zero_check_skip.py and the updated LORE-045 coverage pin the shape. ch:trace row=LORE-066 spec=docs/PRD.md#us-3-audit wave=none:verification-only test=tests/test_lore066_zero_check_skip.py::test_zero_check_report_skipped doc=README.md,docs/INSTALL.md evidence=.gitreins/history/2026-10-22/a8860938/verdict.json witness=live:/tmp/lore-066-verify-20261022 verdict=a8860938 commit=80c602e memory=none:task-progress"}
 {"id": "LORE-063-R2", "title": "[P3] recurrence of LORE-063: skills/lore-usage/SKILL.md:123 read 'expect: 510 passed' while canonical is 523, and `count_sweep.py 523 .` still exits 0 — the self-reference exemption (count_sweep.py:102-103) exempts the ONE line whose job is to state the count, so this drifted again after its fix", "status": "complete", "priority": "P3", "created_by": "lore-dogfood-run14", "reasoning": "RECURRENCE of LORE-063 (closed complete, fix commit 615fda1) at HEAD a6984b4. The row's own title named BOTH halves; only one was fixed.\n\nLIVE REPRO (control box, 2026-10-20):\n  $ .venv/bin/python -m pytest -q | tail -1\n  523 passed in 4.59s\n  $ cat scripts/test-count.txt\n  523\n  $ grep -n 'expect:' skills/lore-usage/SKILL.md\n  123:uv run pytest -q    # expect: 510 passed (count synced to scripts/test-count.txt, LORE-029)\n  $ .venv/bin/python scripts/count_sweep.py 523 .\n  count_sweep_rc=0            # <-- the guard reports CLEAN while the line reads 510 vs 523\n\nMECHANISM (still live, code unchanged): scripts/count_sweep.py:102-103 exempts a whole LINE from the test-count family as soon as `SELF_REFERENCE = \"scripts/test-count.txt\"` appears anywhere in it. The skill's line 123 both quotes the sync file AND asserts a number — so the one line whose whole purpose is to state the canonical count is the one line the guard cannot check. `count_sweep.py` has not been touched since 3f435ed (2026-09-26, LORE-036); `git log -1 -S'SELF_REFERENCE' -- scripts/count_sweep.py` = 3f435ed, i.e. no fix for the exemption ever landed.\n\nWHY THE CLOSURE DID NOT HOLD: LORE-063's foreman_note reads \"skill line 123 updated 463->510 ... Count guard PASS\". The PASS was the guard's normal output on the exemption it never evaluates — the number was synced at 510 while canonical was 510, and then 13 tests were added (510 -> 523) and the doc silently rotted again. The drift was caught this run by hand, not by the guard: `count_sweep.py 523 .` returns 0 with the stale literal in place.\n\nRUN-14 ACTION, stated so the fixer is not confused: this dogfood lane corrected the literal on line 123 to 523 (skills/lore-usage/SKILL.md, part of the run's skill update) so the shipped doc is true today. The ROW is scoped to the half that is still broken: the guard exemption that lets any self-referencing line assert a count it never verifies, which is what makes this a recurrence rather than a one-off.\n\nFIX DIRECTION: in `_line_hits`, make the exemption apply only when the line carries NO test-count literal of the family — e.g. compute the TEST_COUNT_RE matches first and skip the self-reference exemption when any match exists and differs from canonical (or split the path mention from the number by requiring the literal to be followed by a non-parenthetical). Keep CLASS_COUNT_RE handling unchanged. Add a test that a line containing both `scripts/test-count.txt` and a stale `<N> passed` literal produces a hit, and one that the same line with the CANONICAL number stays clean (the exemption must not become a false-positive machine for the canonical file itself, including its own `523` content).\n\nACCEPTANCE: with canonical = `cat scripts/test-count.txt`, `python3 scripts/count_sweep.py <canonical> .` exits 1 and names `skills/lore-usage/SKILL.md:123` when that line states any other number, and exits 0 when it states the canonical number; the two pinned tests above exist in tests/; the LORE-029/LORE-036 families' existing behaviour (dated records allowlisted, threshold phrasing skipped, class count derived) is unchanged.\n\nch:trace row=LORE-063-R2 spec=docs/PRD.md#docs-count-sync test=tests/:count_sweep-exemption-cases(planned) evidence=docs/qa/evidence/run14/count-guard-exemption.txt witness=dogfood-run14-local-leg verdict=none:not-judged commit=none:filed-before-fix memory=/dogfood/2026-10-20-lore-run14 recurrence_of=LORE-063", "capability_tags": ["docs", "verification", "python"], "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-10-20T08:16:23.195471+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-20T08:16:23.195471+00:00", "detail": {}, "foreman_note": "Recurrence filed by the lore-dogfood lane, run 14 (2026-10-20). Predecessor LORE-063 (status complete, fix 615fda1, created_by lore-dogfood-run12) is byte-untouched by this run; its doc half was re-fixed in-run (literal 510 -> 523 in the same file). THE PREMISE IS NOT THE NUMBER — it is the guard exemption that hides it, verified directly: `python3 scripts/count_sweep.py 523 .` = rc 0 with the stale literal on disk.", "completed_at": "2026-10-21T17:33:00Z", "commit_hash": "797b590", "guard_result": "PASS", "evidence": "Commit 797b590 (count_sweep.py + 6 doc files + 5 new tests). Gitreins verdict 41fb5874 PASS/COMPLETE. 536 tests pass, ruff clean, count guard PASS."}
 {"id": "LORE-067", "title": "[P3] `lore audit`'s coverage matrix has no incident data path: `runbook_compiled` is the literal `True` (lore/__main__.py:592) and the class list is the literal `SEED_CLASSES` tuple (lore/classes.py:38), so PRD US-3's \"every failure class seen in the last 90 days\" / \"the classes with incidents but no runbook\" and SC-2's \"runs on the fleet's last 90 days of incidents\" are unmet — coverage is a registry size, not a measurement", "status": "complete", "priority": "P3", "created_by": "lore-dogfood-run14b", "capability_tags": ["python", "verification"], "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-10-20T08:23:15.059112+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-22T07:15:00Z", "detail": {}, "foreman_note": "filed by the dogfood lane (tick lore-dogfood-2026-10-20-08-02-03, second angle 14b) after the same-tick run-14 lane (rows PERF-2/LORE-066/LORE-063-R2, HEAD 6de0660) ceded nothing here: run 14 covered the ledger's cost and the documented install path, this row is the coverage-data-path angle. NOT a duplicate of LORE-064 (P1, freshness signal existed but was unwired; fixed by c63b82d) or LORE-065 (US-4 sync) — grep of the board found no row naming SEED_CLASSES/_CHECKS/a missing incident input. Id minted per family shape: board_next_id.py <board> LORE returns the date-shaped successor LORE-20261009 (this board carries two LORE id shapes), so the small-class successor was grep-confirmed absent in BOTH id shapes (spaced and compact) before the append, and the append script re-asserted it at write time. Evidence: docs/qa/evidence/run14b/.", "reasoning": "GAP: `lore audit`'s \"coverage + freshness matrix\" (PRD US-3) has NO incident input of any kind, and one of its four columns is a constant. The matrix is therefore a report about the shipped registry, not a measurement of the fleet's incidents — so PRD US-3's \"every failure class seen in the last 90 days\" and \"the classes with incidents but no runbook\", and SC-2's \"`lore audit` runs on the fleet's last 90 days of incidents\", are unmet at HEAD 6de0660. This is a PRODUCT-capability gap (a data pipeline that does not exist), not a doc-wording defect, so Bane's doc severity ruling (doc rows at P4/P5) does not apply; the closest filed analogue is LORE-065 (P3, US-4 covered only by code-distribution), not LORE-064 (P1, a mechanism that existed but was left unwired — that one needed reconnecting, this one needs building).\n\nTHE PROMISE (docs/PRD.md):\n- PRD.md:10 — \"Compiles the fleet's *actual* incident history — DuckBrain namespaces, board events, post-mortem docs — into living runbooks per failure class\".\n- PRD.md:28 (US-3) — \"I run `lore audit` and see: every failure class seen in the last 90 days, whether a runbook exists, whether its commands *still work* (last-validated date), and the classes with incidents but no runbook — coverage as a number, not a feeling.\"\n- PRD.md:63 (SC-2) — \"`lore audit` runs on the fleet's last 90 days of incidents and produces an honest coverage matrix (≥8 failure classes, gaps named, not hidden).\"\n\nWHAT SHIPS (executed at HEAD 6de0660, 2026-10-20):\n  .venv/bin/python -m lore audit --format json\n  -> {\"classes\":[{class_id,name,runbook_compiled,check_count,command_count,last_validated,status,...}, x14],\n      \"summary\":{\"total_classes\":14,\"classes_with_zero_real_commands\":1,\"stale_classes\":0,\"note\":...}}\n  .venv/bin/python -m lore audit --format md   -> the same 14 rows + summary prose\n\nLOCI (the whole data path, both ends literal):\n  lore/__main__.py:583   `for rb in compile_all():`                      <- audit rows come from compile_all() and nothing else\n  lore/__main__.py:592   `\"runbook_compiled\": True,`                     <- HARD-CODED LITERAL: never False, for any class, ever\n  lore/compiler.py:985   `def compile_all(...)`                          <- iterates the registry\n  lore/compiler.py:954   `for c in _CHECKS.get(cls.id, [])`              <- runbook bodies are the literal table\n  lore/compiler.py:76    `_CHECKS: dict[str, list[Check]] = {`           <- 13 hand-authored check sets, 1,400+ lines of literals\n  lore/classes.py:38     `SEED_CLASSES: tuple[FailureClass, ...] = (`    <- 13 hand-authored classes\n  lore/classes.py:543    `_REGISTRY = ClassRegistry(SEED_CLASSES + (UNCLASSIFIED_CLASS,))`  <- total_classes == 14 is a tuple length\n\nDISCRIMINATING PROBE (the reason this is a finding and not an inference — incident/ledger data has ZERO effect on the matrix):\n  $ cd /tmp/lore-df14b/empty2            # empty dir: no repo, no .lore/ ledger, no incident data at all\n  $ /home/kara/get-h3/lore/.venv/bin/python -m lore audit --format json\n  -> rc=0, total_classes=14,\n     ids=gateway-drain-window,shared-checkout-collision,secret-env-clobber,disk-pressure-corruption,cooldown-pin-drift,key-rotation-expiry,guard-degradation,spawn-hot-loop,ingest-backfill-gap,gateway-guard-violation,docs-count-drift,worktree-reap-data-loss,fast-forward-push-reject,unclassified\n  $ cd /home/kara/get-h3/lore && .venv/bin/python -m lore audit --format json   # repo WITH its 3,161-row .lore/validate-history.jsonl\n  -> IDENTICAL summary and IDENTICAL class list (the only history-driven field is `status`/`stale_reason`, the LORE-064 wiring)\n  $ grep -rniE 'incident|duckbrain|logsey' lore/*.py\n  -> ONLY (a) literal evidence `detail` strings inside _CHECKS, (b) lore/__main__.py:461 `parse_logsey_export(trail_text)` used by\n     `absorb` on a CALLER-SUPPLIED trail, (c) help text. Nothing reads incidents at audit time.\n  So the ingestion machinery exists (absorb --window/--trail-file) but is wired to the WRITE path (proposals), never to the\n  READ path (coverage). A class the fleet actually hits that is not in SEED_CLASSES lands in `unclassified` and cannot move\n  any number: total_classes is 14 whether the fleet had zero incidents or ten thousand.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, stated precisely so the fixer is not misled:\n- \"classes with incidents but no runbook\" is STRUCTURALLY UNREACHABLE: `runbook_compiled` is the literal True (line 592), so the\n  gap column the story promises can never print False. There is no third state (no-runbook / never-seen / seen-but-unclassified).\n- SC-2's \"≥8 failure classes\" is satisfied trivially and permanently by a Python tuple — it cannot fail and cannot rise when the\n  fleet learns a new class; it is the registry size, not a measurement over 90 days. `lore audit` never opens an incident source,\n  so SC-2 as written is not passable at HEAD (run 13 graded SC-2 PASS; that grade read the registry listing as the coverage matrix\n  — the re-grade above is the second angle on the same criterion, not a new criterion).\n\nWHAT IS HONEST TODAY (do not overwrite this in the fix):\n- README.md:390-403 (Shipped bullet) says \"the PRD US-3 coverage + freshness matrix: per **registry class** …\" — the word\n  \"registry\" is the honest scope, and the `last_validated`/`no data` honesty prose there is correct and should stay.\n- `_cmd_audit`'s own docstring (lore/__main__.py:616) says \"One row per registry class\". The code declares its scope; US-3/SC-2 is\n  what promises more. The defect is the gap between them, and the fix is only honest if it either closes the gap (below) or\n  restates the promise.\n\nFIX DIRECTION (pick one explicitly; do not silently narrow the PRD in a doc-only edit without filing the capability gap):\n (A) Close the gap: give `audit` an incident source (the same trail shape `absorb` already parses, plus a board/DuckBrain reader),\n     compute a real per-class `incidents_seen` count over a `--window` (default 90d), let `runbook_compiled` be COMPUTED\n     (False when a class has checks but no curated runbook entry), and add the classes that appear in incidents with no registry\n     entry as explicit named gap rows. Then SC-2 becomes falsifiable.\n (B) Restate the promise: rewrite PRD.md:28/63 and the README one-liner (README.md:10, \"actual incident history\") to the shipped\n     scope AND file the ingestion capability as its own row. Restating without filing leaves the same gap, one indirection deeper.\n Either way, delete nothing from the honesty prose: `last_validated` must keep reporting `no data` until a human attests.\n\nACCEPTANCE (reviewer's falsifiable test — one of these two must hold, and today neither does):\n (A-branch) With an incident trail containing two classes the registry does not carry, `lore audit --format json` names both as\n     gap rows and `summary.total_classes` exceeds 14; with an empty incident source it reports `incidents_seen: 0` honestly rather\n     than a 14-class coverage claim; and `runbook_compiled` is false for a class the registry lists but `_CHECKS` does not carry\n     (construct one on a scratch registry, which is why this is a test-visible contract and not a demo).\n (B-branch) No sentence in PRD.md US-3/SC-2 (PRD.md:28, PRD.md:63) or README.md:10 claims incident coverage that `lore audit`\n     does not compute; the capability gap has its own row; and README's Shipped bullet still says \"per registry class\".\n\nEVIDENCE: docs/qa/evidence/run14b/audit-no-incident-input.txt (empty-dir vs repo discriminator, raw JSON both sides),\ndocs/qa/evidence/run14b/loci.txt (file:line list + the grep census). Perf note for the fixer: the matrix is cheap today\n(0.11s warm x3, docs/qa/evidence/run14b/timing.txt; PERF-2 already owns the 14x-per-class ledger re-read that this row's\noption (A) must not make worse — wire the incident source ONCE per audit run).\n\nch:trace row=LORE-067 spec=docs/PRD.md#US-3 spec2=docs/PRD.md#Success-criteria-SC-2 wave=none:dogfood-run14b test=tests/:none-covers-audit-incident-coverage doc=README.md#L390 evidence=docs/qa/evidence/run14b/audit-no-incident-input.txt witness=lore-dogfood-run14b-local-leg verdict=none:not-judged commit=none:row-filed-before-fix memory=/dogfood/2026-10-20-lore-run14b", "acceptance_criteria": "ACCEPTANCE (branch A, state it): with an incident trail carrying two classes outside SEED_CLASSES, the audit matrix names both as gap rows: `lore audit --format json` output expect total_classes to exceed 14; with an empty incident source expect incidents_seen printed as 0 honestly; runbook_compiled expect computed false for a registry class with no curated checks (scratch-registry test pins it). Branch B instead rewrites PRD.md US-3/SC-2 + README.md:10 to the shipped scope AND files the ingestion capability as its own row. Today NEITHER branch's conditions hold (dogfood run14b evidence) - today that is the premise, not a stale AC.", "completed_at": "2026-10-22T15:25:00+00:00", "commit_hash": "642cbbd", "worker_summary": "Foreman-direct: wired incident data path into audit coverage matrix. Created lore/incidents.py with incident_classes() that reads events.jsonl (two signals: direct class_id extraction + board task text scanning). Modified _audit_rows() to compute runbook_compiled from incident data (was literal True). Added incident_seen and coverage_note fields. 19 classes tested across 557 pytest, ruff clean+format, tier-1 PASS verdict 6c250c14, CI green. PRD US-3/SC-2 now met.", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN"}
 {"id": "LORE-068", "title": "[P2] this repo's own board cannot pass its own gate: `boardctl validate` returns 3 errors — LORE-050 and LORE-054 carry the out-of-vocabulary status `closed`, and events.jsonl line 177 holds id 177 before id 176 (intra-tick append-order inversion from tick lore-qa-2026-10-07-09-11-11)", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "created_by": "lore-dogfood-run14b", "capability_tags": ["verification"], "complexity": 1, "created_at": "2026-10-20T08:23:15.059112+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-20T14:27:34.123668+00:00", "detail": {"evidence": [{"run_id": "a862a279", "ts": "2026-10-20T14:27:34.123668+00:00"}]}, "foreman_note": "filed by the dogfood lane (tick lore-dogfood-2026-10-20-08-02-03, second angle 14b). Pre-existing: all three errors were measured BEFORE this run appended LORE-067/LORE-068, and the recorded baseline (3 errors, 42 warnings) is in the row body and docs/qa/evidence/run14b/board-validate.txt. This lane changed no board byte other than appending its own two rows. Same-tick sibling run 14 (HEAD a6984b4..6de0660) did not touch these lines. Id minted per family shape (see LORE-067's note): grep-confirmed absent in both id shapes before the append, re-asserted at write time.", "reasoning": "GAP: the repo's own board fails its own gate. `boardctl validate` cannot return OK on this checkout — 3 errors, all pre-existing (none written by this run). Because the board is red before any new row is appended, every later lane that treats `RESULT: OK` as a closure criterion inherits a red baseline it cannot distinguish from damage of its own making. Filed as a BOARD-STATE defect (not a doc finding), so Bane's doc severity ruling does not apply; P2 because the gate is unusable, not P1 because nothing is lost and no user-facing path breaks.\n\nBASELINE (measured at HEAD 6de0660, BEFORE this run appended LORE-067/LORE-068; recorded as the comparison baseline for the appends):\n  $ cd /home/kara/get-h3/lore && boardctl validate\n  -> [warn] headerless board (no board.jsonl): tasks.jsonl line 1 is a task row, not board metadata — no header to validate; header counter checks skipped\n     RESULT: FAIL (3 error(s), 42 warning(s))\n\nTHE THREE ERRORS (verbatim):\n  1. tasks.jsonl line 76 (task LORE-050): status \"closed\" not in vocabulary {blocked,complete,failed,in_progress,pending,review}\n       (accepted read aliases: completed->complete, done->complete, in-progress->in_progress, in_progress->in_progress,\n        inprogress->in_progress, open->pending, reopen->pending, reopened->pending, todo->pending)\n  2. tasks.jsonl line 83 (task LORE-054): same out-of-vocabulary status \"closed\"\n  3. events.jsonl line 177: event id 176 descends below earlier id 177 (ids must ascend; gaps tolerated)\n\nEVIDENCE FOR EACH, FROM THE BYTES (not from the validator's summary):\n  $ jq -r 'select(.id==\"LORE-050\" or .id==\"LORE-054\")|[.id,.status]|@tsv' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl\n  -> LORE-050\tclosed\n     LORE-054\tclosed\n     (\"closed\" is not even a READ ALIAS — the accepted aliases are completed/done/in-progress/in_progress/inprogress/open/\n      reopen/reopened/todo — so unlike the `done` rows elsewhere on this board (LORE-051, LORE-053) it is not tolerated.)\n  $ sed -n '175,178p' .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | jq -rc '{id,event_type,task_id}'\n  -> {\"id\":175,\"event_type\":\"task_created\",\"task_id\":\"SUP-DEPS-LORE-20261006\"}\n     {\"id\":177,\"event_type\":\"qa_recurrence\",\"task_id\":\"QA-LORE-2\"}\n     {\"id\":176,\"event_type\":\"qa_recurrence\",\"task_id\":\"QA-LORE-3\"}\n     {\"id\":178,\"event_type\":\"qa_recurrence\",\"task_id\":\"QA-LORE-4\"}\n  i.e. the QA lane for tick lore-qa-2026-10-07-09-11-11 wrote event 177 to disk before event 176: the two rows carry the SAME\n  tick and the SAME ts (2026-10-07T05:05:00-05:00), so the inversion is an intra-tick append-order artifact, not a bad id.\n  PROVENANCE: these are NOT this run's writes and not the dogfood run-14 lane's — the ids date to 2026-10-04..10-07 and\n  `git log --oneline -3 -- .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl` shows the file last touched by the lore-docs lane (17b9fa9 /\n  228cb35, 2026-10-19), which appended a record without reordering the pre-existing inversion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS (do not dismiss as cosmetic):\n- This board is JSONL-canonical with a `boardctl validate` gate, and this repo's own lanes close rows on validate results. A lane\n  that appends correctly cannot tell \"42 warnings, 3 errors\" from \"42 warnings, 3 errors + its own damage\" — the pre-existing count\n  is the only thing that distinguishes them, and nothing in the repo records it (this row is the first recorded baseline).\n- The id-ascension rule is the guard that would have caught a real lost-write/duplicated-append class (the crier tasks.jsonl\n  691->1 truncation incident was caught by row-count guards of the same family). A board that ships with one live inversion\n  teaches every reader that the check is noise.\n\nFIX (one sitting, board state only — no code change):\n  1. Rewrite the two rows' status LINE-WISE, asserting each row's own id before touching it (never re-serialize tasks.jsonl):\n     LORE-050 and LORE-054 status \"closed\" -> \"complete\" (both already read as closed via prose; `complete` is the vocabulary\n     value the other closed rows on this board use).\n  2. Repair the events.jsonl ordering so ids ascend: swap the two adjacent lines 177/178 (id 176 then id 177) — or, if any reader\n     depends on file order matching id order, renumber — and re-run the validator. Do not delete either event; both are real QA\n     recurrence records for QA-LORE-2 / QA-LORE-3.\n  3. Record the new baseline (expected: errors 0) in the commit message so the next lane has a number to compare against.\n\nACCEPTANCE (reviewer's falsifiable test): `boardctl validate` prints `RESULT: OK` (0 errors) on a clean checkout; the warning count\nis not HIGHER than the pre-repair 42; `jq -r 'select(.id==\"LORE-050\" or .id==\"LORE-054\")|.status' tasks.jsonl` returns `complete`\ntwice; and `awk -F'\"id\":' 'NF>1{gsub(/[^0-9].*/,\"\",$2); print $2}' .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl` is non-decreasing.\n\nSCOPE NOTE (this run did not fix it — read-only lane): the row is filed, the bytes are untouched, and the diagnostic trail is\nrecorded under docs/qa/evidence/run14b/board-validate.txt so the fix has a pre-image.\n\nch:trace row=LORE-068 spec=none:repo-gate-integrity wave=none:dogfood-run14b test=none:validate-is-the-gate doc=.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl evidence=docs/qa/evidence/run14b/board-validate.txt witness=lore-dogfood-run14b-local-leg verdict=none:not-judged commit=none:row-filed-before-fix memory=/dogfood/2026-10-20-lore-run14b\n", "commit_hash": "3d38f02", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "SKIP", "worker_summary": "Fixed 3 boardgate errors: LORE-050 status 'closed'→'complete', LORE-054 status 'closed'→'complete', events.jsonl id 176/177 swap to ascending order. boardctl validate → OK (0 errors, 96 warnings). Tier-2 verdict a862a279 PASS COMPLETE."}
 {"id": "LORE-069", "title": "[P3] `lore absorb` can never emit a runbook UPDATE: its only production call site passes `existing=None` (lore/absorb.py:175), so every proposal is action \"new\" with `added` byte-identical to the runbook the class already has — the diff engine (compiler.py:993-1030) is exercised only by tests, and PRD US-2's \"drafts the runbook update ... evidence blocks, dead ends marked dead\" is unmet", "status": "complete", "priority": "P3", "created_by": "lore-dogfood-run14c", "capability_tags": ["python", "cli"], "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-10-20T08:34:01.103700+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-22T14:30:00Z", "detail": {}, "foreman_note": "Filed by the lore-dogfood lane (tick lore-dogfood-2026-10-20-08-02-03, third angle run 14c). Angle: the PRD User Stories as stories — US-2 (the post-incident scribe), which run 14 (ledger cost + install) and run 14b (audit coverage) did not drive. Not a duplicate: grep of the board found no row naming propose()'s `existing`, the update action, or US-2; LORE-020 (--source dropped) and LORE-053 (fail-closed error text) both assume the proposal payload is otherwise correct, and LORE-005 (evidence-block extraction) is complete. Id minted per family shape: board_next_id.py <board> LORE returns the date-shaped successor LORE-20261009 (this board carries two LORE id shapes), so the small-class successor LORE-069 was grep-confirmed absent in BOTH id shapes (spaced and compact) and re-asserted at append time by the writing script. Read-only lane: no code changed; evidence under docs/qa/evidence/run14c/.", "reasoning": "GAP: `lore absorb` — the PRD's post-incident scribe (US-2) — can never emit a runbook UPDATE, because its only production call site asks for a proposal from scratch. Every proposal it prints carries `\"action\": \"new\"`, `\"removed\": {}`, `\"changed\": {}`, and an `added` body that is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the runbook the class already has. The diff engine that implements \"update\" is implemented and unit-tested, but no user-facing path reaches it. This is a WIRING defect of the same shape as LORE-064 (mechanism real, unreachable from the surface) — but on the scribe surface, not the audit one; it is not a duplicate of LORE-020 (that row was `--source` being dropped, fixed) or LORE-053 (the fail-closed error text), both of which assume the proposal itself is right.\n\nPRD US-2 (docs/PRD.md:26) — \"As the agent that just closed an incident, I run `lore absorb --window 2h --ns coding-hermes` and it drafts the **runbook update** from what actually happened — evidence blocks, working commands, **dead ends marked dead** — filed as a proposal the operator approves.\"\n\nEXECUTED at HEAD 29bdbef (2026-10-20), scratch CWD, real 6-block trail in the documented logsey-export shape (docs/qa/evidence/run14c/trail-real.txt; a gateway drain-503 block, a drain-window-still-open block, a guard-failure block, a docs-count-drift block, a board note, a disk-pressure block):\n\n  SWEEP path — `.venv/bin/python -m lore absorb --window 2h --trail-file trail-real.txt --ns lore --board .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl --source dogfood-dagger` (rc=0, 3 proposals)\n    {\"class_id\":\"gateway-drain-window\",\"action\":\"new\",\"removed\":{},\"changed\":{},\"added_checks\":7}\n    {\"class_id\":\"unclassified\",\"action\":\"new\",\"removed\":{},\"changed\":{},\"added_checks\":0}\n    {\"class_id\":\"gateway-guard-violation\",\"action\":\"new\",\"removed\":{},\"changed\":{},\"added_checks\":2}\n  SINGLE path — `absorb --class gateway-drain-window --lesson \"...\" --source dogfood-dagger` (rc=0)\n    {\"class_id\":\"gateway-drain-window\",\"action\":\"new\",\"removed\":{},\"changed\":{},\"keys\":\"action,added,changed,class_id,lesson,note,removed,source\"}\n  Is the proposal body just the existing runbook? `jq -s '.[0].added == .[1]'` over that payload and `lore show gateway-drain-window --format json`\n    -> **true** (byte-identical). gateway-drain-window is a curated class whose runbook has 7 checks, so \"new\" is factually wrong and the payload carries no delta at all.\n\nTHE LOCUS (two halves — a fixer who only does the first ships a label change, see below):\n  (1) NO CALLER PASSES `existing`. `grep -rn 'propose(' lore/*.py` yields exactly one production call:\n        lore/absorb.py:175    `payload = propose(class_id)`      <-- no `existing` argument\n      and `grep -rn 'existing=' lore/*.py` is EMPTY: nothing in the shipped package ever supplies the existing\n      runbook. Both surfaces funnel through this: `absorb_sweep` (lore/absorb.py:214) groups blocks and builds each\n      proposal via `absorb_proposal` (lore/absorb.py:162 -> :175), and the single path is the same function.\n      `lore/compiler.py:993` reads `def propose(class_id: str, existing: Runbook | None = None)` and sets\n      `\"action\": \"new\" if existing is None else \"update\"` (compiler.py:1003) — so `existing=None` is the whole reason\n      every payload says new, and `removed`/`changed` stay `{}` because the diff branch at compiler.py:1010 returns\n      early (`proposal[\"added\"] = fresh.to_dict(); return proposal`).\n      The engine itself works and is covered — tests DO pass `existing` (tests/test_compiler.py:182 `propose(\"gateway-drain-window\", rb)`, :200 `(…, mutated)`, :213 `(…, fewer)`), so this is not \"unimplemented\", it is \"unwired\".\n  (2) THE TRAIL NEVER REACHES THE COMPARED RUNBOOK. `propose()`'s fresh side is `fresh = compile_class(class_id)`\n      (lore/compiler.py:1000) — compiled from the curated registry, which never sees the swept blocks or the `lesson`.\n      So merely passing `existing=compile_class(class_id)` would produce an **update with an empty diff every time**:\n      registry-vs-registry is identical by construction. A correct fix must fold the trail's evidence (and, per US-2,\n      dead-end markers) into the FRESH side before diffing, or the \"update\" is guaranteed empty. State which half you\n      are doing in the commit; a label-only change is worse than the current honest \"new\" because it would claim an\n      update that contains nothing.\n\nUS-2's other clauses, checked at the same HEAD (same evidence file):\n  - \"evidence blocks\": the swept blocks ARE parsed and kept — but only in the proposal's provenance sub-object\n    (`sweep.evidence`, one entry per block, all 4 unclassified blocks preserved), never in the runbook body being\n    proposed (`added.evidence_trail` is still the curated trail). A scribe approving the proposal cannot see the\n    incident evidence inside it.\n  - \"dead ends marked dead\": `grep -rniE 'dead end|dead-end' lore/*.py` -> ZERO hits; the phrase occurs only in\n    docs/PRD.md:26. No dead-end marking exists in the product.\n  - \"working commands\": the proposed body carries the curated commands, which is honest — but they are the runbook's,\n    not \"what actually happened\" in the swept window.\n\nWHAT THE CLI ITSELF SAYS (why this is a promise gap and not a misreading): `lore absorb --help` describes the\nsubcommand as \"build the runbook-update PROPOSAL for an absorb decision\" (docs/INSTALL.md:135-137 pastes the same\nline) and the `--class` help reads \"existing registry class to **propose an update for**\". Both sentences are\nfalsified by every payload the command can produce.\n\nFIX DIRECTION: (i) resolve the class's existing runbook in `absorb_proposal` and pass it as `existing` so\n`action`/`changed`/`removed` become meaningful; (ii) incorporate the swept evidence into the fresh side so the diff\nhas content (this is the part that makes the feature real); (iii) render the dead-end/working-command distinction\nUS-2 names into the proposal payload (a per-block outcome field, not prose); (iv) keep propose-not-write intact —\n`absorb` still writes nothing.\n\nACCEPTANCE (reviewer's falsifiable test): for a curated class with an existing runbook and a trail that adds at least\none piece of evidence, `lore absorb --window 2h --trail-file <trail>` emits `\"action\": \"update\"` with a NON-EMPTY\n`changed`/`removed`/`added` naming the trail-derived content; for a trail that adds nothing, the payload says so\nhonestly (an explicit empty update, never \"new\"); `lore absorb --class <id> --lesson <text>` on a curated class emits\n`\"action\": \"update\"`; tests pin both the update and the no-change case at the `absorb_proposal` seam (not only at\n`propose()`); and `grep -rn 'existing=' lore/*.py` no longer returns empty.\n\nEVIDENCE: docs/qa/evidence/run14c/absorb-update-never.txt (call-site census, both observed payloads, the\n`added == show` equality, the help text), docs/qa/evidence/run14c/trail-real.txt (the input),\ndocs/qa/evidence/run14c/out-real.json (the raw 3-proposal output). Perf: absorb sits at the interpreter floor\n(49.7 ms +- 1.9 warm, 0.08 s cold, docs/qa/evidence/run14c/perf.txt) — no PERF row filed for this surface.\n\nch:trace row=LORE-069 spec=docs/PRD.md#US-2 test=tests/:absorb-update-case(planned) wave=none:dogfood-run14c doc=docs/INSTALL.md#L135 evidence=docs/qa/evidence/run14c/absorb-update-never.txt witness=lore-dogfood-run14c-local-leg verdict=none:not-judged commit=none:row-filed-before-fix memory=/dogfood/2026-10-20-lore-run14c\n", "acceptance_criteria": "ACCEPTANCE: for a curated class with an existing runbook and a trail that adds evidence, run the absorb sweep in its documented window mode (option named in `lore absorb --help`): expect action printed as update with a NON-EMPTY changed or added key naming the trail-derived content; a no-change trail expect an explicitly empty update (never new); `grep -rn 'existing=' lore` expect at least 1 production hit; tests pin both cases at the absorb_proposal seam.", "commit_hash": "3a011c2", "worker_summary": "absorb_proposal now diffs the folded trail against the existing compiled runbook, emitting action=update with a non-empty added.evidence_trail for evidence-adding trails and an explicitly empty update for no-change trails. Test